\description{
Drops the small components of a regression forest's functional decomposition and, optionally, refits one
coefficient per kept component. The rule and the threshold are chosen by K-fold cross-validation on the training
rows.
}
\details{
The forest is decomposed into its purified components \code{f_S}, one per set \code{S} of predictors (see \code{\link[=purify]{purify()}} and
\code{\link[=predict_components]{predict_components()}}), at all orders up to \code{max_interaction}, and its intercept \code{f_0}. The size of a component is
its share of the variance of the prediction \code{f} on the training rows, \code{share_S = Var(f_S) / Var(f)}. The shares are
descriptive sizes, not a partition: the components are not orthogonal on the data, so they need not add up to 1. A
component is kept if its share is at least \code{tau} (and it is not identically zero on the training rows). Two rules
are available:
\itemize{
\item \code{"D"} (drop): the prediction is \code{f_0} plus the kept components. \code{rule = "D"} with \code{tau = 0} is the unpruned
forest.
\item \code{"R"} (drop and refit): the prediction is \code{alpha + sum(beta_S * f_S)} over the kept components, with the
intercept \code{alpha} and the coefficients \code{beta_S} by least squares of \code{y} on the kept components over the training
rows (the minimum-norm solution if the components are collinear). The components themselves are not changed,
only their scale.
}

\strong{Cross-validation.} If more than one pair of \code{rule} and \code{tau} is given (the default: both rules and the grid
0, 0.01\%, 0.03\%, 0.1\%, 0.3\%, 1\%, 3\%, 10\%), the pair is chosen by K-fold cross-validation on the training rows. For
each fold, the forest is refitted with the parameters of \code{object} on the other folds (after \code{set.seed(seed)}, the
same for every fold), its components, shares and refit are computed on those rows, and every candidate is scored
on the held-out fold. The CV RMSE is \code{sqrt(sum(SSE_k) / n)}, summing the squared errors over the K folds. With
\code{select = "min"}, the pair with the lowest CV RMSE is chosen (ties, up to a relative \code{1e-12}: the larger \code{tau},
then \code{"D"}). With \code{select = "1se"}, the largest \code{tau} whose CV MSE is within one standard error of the minimum is
chosen (the SE is the standard deviation of the per-fold MSEs of the minimum, divided by \code{sqrt(K)}), and at that
\code{tau} the rule with the lower CV RMSE.

The chosen pair is then applied to \code{object} itself (not refitted): its components and shares on \code{data}, and for
\code{"R"} the least squares on \code{data}. \code{data} and \code{y} must therefore be the rows \code{object} was trained on, in the order
of the fit (checked).

\strong{Purification.} \code{object} is purified in place at all orders, as \code{\link[=purify]{purify()}} does, if it is not purified yet. This
does not change its predictions. A forest purified with a \code{maxp_interaction} below its order is purified again at
all orders, with a message: the pruning needs every component.

\strong{Random numbers.} The folds are drawn after \code{set.seed(seed)}, and the fold fits after it too. Afterwards the
random number stream is restored to its state on entry (after drawing \code{seed} if it is \code{NULL}), so the call does not
reset the caller's stream.

\strong{Cost.} The CV fits and purifies the forest K more times. The components are evaluated on the training rows:
only those the purified forest stores (at most \code{sum(choose(p, seq_len(max_interaction)))}, usually far fewer: the
subsets of the trees' predictor sets), in one pass over the rows. The memory is about \code{n} doubles per stored
component. \code{nthreads} speeds up the fits and the purification.

Only regression forests are supported.
